  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 130 AM EDT Mon Oct 26 2026\n\nA weak convergent boundary is noted on radar mosaic and surface obs \njust to our northwest this morning. This front is associated with an \nupper low crossing Hudson Bay and is becoming increasingly diffuse \nas the parent forcing zips east and strong mid/upper ridge holds \nover the Ohio Valley. No rain is expected locally, just a steady \nstream of high clouds given incredibly dry and stable low levels. \nOur area does see a wind shift later today into tonight to NNE and \neventually east. Will have to keep an eye on fog potential given \nreturn of Lake Erie contribution but ample high clouds may limit \ndevelopment as is occurring now. Highs today still reach low 70s \nahead of the front and flow veers back to a more southerly direction \nby Tue afternoon so highs on Tue are still around 70 (slightly \ncooler far NE).\n\nS/SW flow and warm/moist air advection ramp up again Tue night \nthrough Thu as another front take aims at the region. A more \nsouthward track of the parent trough and a much better prefrontal \nLLJ/theta-e surge suggest at least some chances for rain on Thu. The \ntrough is still well to the north though and local areas remain \nrelatively dry and stable. Best chances for some light rain will be \nin our far NW Thu afternoon. Latest NBM even throws in a chance for \nthunder in our NW but not expecting anything significant and areas \nalong/south of US-24 likely remain dry. Highs do climb near 80 on \nThu, which is the record (set in 2024).\n\nUpper ridge finally breaks down this weekend with potential for a \nsouthern stream trough to tap into better moisture and bring our \nbest chances for rain in a while. Still a ton of spread regarding \nthe timing and evolution though. Halloween still looks mostly dry \nbut NBM is maintaining some slight chance PoPs. Better chances \narrive Sunday into Monday but confidence on timing and amounts \ncouldn't be much lower.",
